   "source": [
    "# 03.1 - Descriptive Statistics\n",
    "\n",
    "**Phase:** 03 - Statistics & Probability\n",
    "**Difficulty:** Beginner\n",
    "**Priority:** High — every ML pipeline starts here\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## What Are We Solving?\n",
    "\n",
    "Before any modeling, we need to **understand our data**. Descriptive statistics summarize a dataset with a few key numbers: where the data is centered (mean, median, mode), how spread out it is (variance, standard deviation, range), and its shape (quartiles, skewness). Without this step, you are building models blind.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why Does It Matter?\n",
    "\n",
    "Every ML project starts with **exploratory data analysis (EDA)**. Descriptive statistics reveal outliers, skewness, and scale issues that must be handled before training a model. They also tell you which model assumptions might be violated. A famous example: the Titanic dataset has missing ages for many passengers. If you just filled them with the mean, you'd get a reasonable but biased result. The median would be more robust because the age distribution is skewed. Skipping this step leads to garbage-in-garbage-out models.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Mental Model\n",
    "\n",
    "Think of descriptive statistics as a **medical checkup** for your data. The **mean** is your average heart rate — it tells you the center but can be thrown off by a single sprint. The **median** is your resting heart rate — more robust. The **standard deviation** is how much your heart rate varies throughout the day. **Skewness** tells you if you have occasional spikes (right-skewed) or dips (left-skewed). Just like a doctor wouldn't diagnose you from one number, you shouldn't model your data from one statistic.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Prerequisites\n",
    "\n",
    "- Phase 02 (Mathematics for ML)\n",
    "- Unit 01.6 (Pandas)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Learning Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "By the end of this notebook, you should be able to:\n",
    "- [ ] Compute measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode)\n",
    "- [ ] Compute measures of spread (variance, std, range, IQR)\n",
    "- [ ] Understand quartiles and percentiles\n",
    "- [ ] Detect skewness and outliers\n",
    "- [ ] Use NumPy and Pandas to summarize data\n",
    "- [ ] Know when to use mean vs median\n",
    "- [ ] Apply descriptive statistics in EDA\n",
    "\n",
    "## Statistical Intuition Before Formulas\n",
    "\n",
    "The **mean** is the balance point — if you put your data on a seesaw, the mean is where it balances. The **median** is the middle value — half the data is above, half below. The **mode** is the most popular value. When the mean and median differ, the data is **skewed** — the mean gets pulled toward the tail. This is why reports of average income are misleading: a few billionaires pull the mean up, while the median represents what a \"typical\" person earns.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **variance** measures how far data points are from the mean, on average (squared). The **standard deviation** is just the square root of variance, putting it back in the original units. The **IQR** (interquartile range) is the range of the middle 50% — it's robust to outliers, unlike the range.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Core Concepts\n",
    "\n",
    "### Central Tendency: Mean, Median, Mode\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Mean**: the arithmetic average — sensitive to outliers.\n",
    "- **Median**: the middle value when sorted — robust to outliers.\n",
    "- **Mode**: the most frequent value — useful for categorical data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's compute these on a small dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "e252fda7",
   "metadata": {
