   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "\n",
    "# Small dataset with an outlier\n",
    "data = np.array([3, 5, 7, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, 100])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Mean: balance point of the data\n",
    "mean = np.mean(data)\n",
    "# Median: middle value (robust to outliers)\n",
    "median = np.median(data)\n",
    "# Mode: most frequent value\n",
    "mode = stats.mode(data).mode\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Data: {data}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Mean:   {mean:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Median: {median:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Mode:   {mode}\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nNote: the mean (17.6) is much larger than the median (8.5)\")\n",
    "print(\"because of the outlier 100. The median is robust to outliers.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e7b32931",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Measures of Spread\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Range**: max - min (sensitive to outliers).\n",
    "- **Variance**: average squared deviation from the mean.\n",
    "- **Standard deviation**: square root of variance — in the same units as the data.\n",
    "- **IQR** (interquartile range): 75th percentile - 25th percentile — robust measure of spread.\n",
    "\n",
    "Variance formula (population): `Var = (1/n) * sum((x - mean)^2)`\n",
    "\n",
    "Sample variance uses `1/(n-1)` (Bessel's correction) to be unbiased. The idea: when you estimate the mean from the sample itself, you lose one degree of freedom, so dividing by n-1 compensates."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
